            "<strong>Definition and purpose.</strong> Oxygen is a drug given to correct hypoxaemia — not breathlessness, not anxiety, not ‘just in case’ — and like every drug it needs a <strong>prescription with a target saturation range</strong>, a device, a flow, monitoring and review: the target is <strong>94–98 per cent</strong> for most patients and <strong>88–92 per cent</strong> for those at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, severe kyphoscoliosis or neuromuscular disease, morbid obesity, bronchiectasis with carbon dioxide retention), who carry an alert card or a note on the chart; in children the World Health Organization threshold for starting oxygen is a saturation <strong>below 90 per cent</strong> (below 94 per cent in severe illness), and in a newborn the target in the first days is set by the neonatal unit. Too little oxygen damages the brain and heart; too much is harmful in the retainer, in myocardial infarction and stroke with normal saturations, and in the premature infant's eyes and lungs, which is why ‘15 litres for everyone’ is no longer practice outside the resuscitation room. <strong>Requirements:</strong> the prescription; a working source (piped outlet with flowmeter, cylinder with regulator and contents gauge, or <strong>concentrator</strong> — the mainstay of district hospitals in Ghana, delivering up to 5 or 10 L/minute at 85–95 per cent oxygen as long as it has power and a clean filter, with a cylinder as backup for the power cut); the device sized for the patient; sterile water and a humidifier for high flows or prolonged use; a pulse oximeter; the oxygen chart.",
            "<strong>Devices and flows.</strong> <strong>Nasal cannula</strong> at 1–4 L/minute gives roughly 24–40 per cent, lets the patient eat, drink and talk, and is the device of choice for most ward patients and for children (<strong>0.5–1 L/minute in neonates, 1–2 L/minute in infants and up to 4 L/minute in older children</strong>, prongs that fit the nostrils without blanching the skin); above 4 L/minute it dries and bleeds the nose. A <strong>simple face mask</strong> at 5–10 L/minute gives 40–60 per cent and is <strong>never run below 5 L/minute</strong>, because exhaled carbon dioxide then accumulates in the mask and is rebreathed. A <strong>non-rebreathing reservoir mask</strong> at 10–15 L/minute gives 60–90 per cent for emergencies — the reservoir bag is <strong>inflated with a finger over the valve before the mask goes on</strong> and must not collapse on inspiration. A <strong>Venturi mask</strong> delivers an exact fixed concentration — 24 per cent (blue, 2–4 L/minute), 28 per cent (white, 4–6), 35 per cent (yellow, 8–10), 40 per cent (red, 10–12), 60 per cent (green, 12–15) — and is the device for the retainer, where precision matters; the flow written on the valve is the minimum, and a tachypnoeic patient needs more flow, not a different valve. <strong>Humidification</strong> (a bubble humidifier with sterile water, changed daily, or heated humidification) is used for flows above 4 L/minute, for more than 24 hours, for tracheostomy and for thick secretions — dry oxygen crusts and blocks. <strong>Steps:</strong> check the prescription and target; explain and consent (the mask frightens children and the confused — a parent's lap and a cannula work better); assemble, set the flow and confirm it at the patient end (feel it, see the bag inflate); position upright; fit the device without pressure on the ears, bridge of the nose or cheeks (pad the straps, check the skin every four hours); <strong>record device, flow and saturation, with the respiratory rate and the level of consciousness</strong>, at least four-hourly and within five minutes of any change; <strong>wean</strong> by stepping the flow down while the saturation holds in the target range, stop when it is held on room air, and recheck five minutes after stopping and again at an hour. Safety: no naked flame, oil, grease or petroleum-based lip balm near oxygen; cylinders upright, secured, turned off when not in use, and the contents gauge read before transport — <strong>cylinder contents in litres = gauge pressure in bar × cylinder water capacity in litres</strong>, so a full E-size cylinder (about 4.7 L capacity at 137 bar) holds roughly <strong>680 litres</strong> and lasts <strong>680 ÷ 15 = 45 minutes</strong> at 15 L/minute, and a half-full one about 22 minutes — a calculation done before every transfer, not after the gauge hits red in the lift. Pulse oximetry is read with its limitations in mind: poor perfusion, movement, nail varnish and bright light give false readings, carbon monoxide poisoning gives a falsely normal one, and <strong>oximeters overestimate saturation by two to three points in darkly pigmented skin</strong>, so a reading of 92 per cent in a Ghanaian patient may be 89, and the clinical picture and a blood gas decide when the numbers and the patient disagree.",
