            "<strong>Positioning</strong> comes first: the breathless conscious patient sits upright or leans forward on a table; the unconscious breathing patient goes into the <strong>recovery position</strong>; an obstructed airway is opened by <strong>head tilt–chin lift</strong>, or <strong>jaw thrust</strong> without head tilt where the cervical spine may be injured; a child's large occiput is managed with a neutral position (a folded towel under the shoulders in an infant) rather than full extension, which kinks the soft trachea. An <strong>oropharyngeal (Guedel) airway</strong> is used only in a patient <strong>without a gag reflex</strong> — in one who gags it provokes vomiting and aspiration, and a patient who pushes it out does not need it — and is <strong>sized from the incisors (or the corner of the mouth) to the angle of the jaw</strong>: too long presses the epiglottis down, too short pushes the tongue back. In the adult it is inserted <strong>upside down along the hard palate and rotated 180 degrees</strong> as the tip reaches the soft palate; in a child it goes in the right way up over a tongue depressor, because rotation damages the soft palate. A <strong>nasopharyngeal airway</strong> is tolerated by the semi-conscious patient with a gag, is sized by <strong>length from the nostril to the tragus</strong> (the diameter-of-the-little-finger rule is unreliable), is lubricated and passed bevel towards the septum straight back along the floor of the nose with a gentle twist, and is <strong>avoided in suspected base-of-skull fracture</strong> (bruising around the eyes or behind the ears, cerebrospinal fluid from the nose) and in bleeding disorders. The airway is secured only with the patient's own tolerance, never taped in, and removed when the gag returns.",
