          alt: "After the operation: what goes wrong, and on which day. Post-operative complications keep a timetable. First hours: airway, bleeding, shock, pain. Day 1 to 2: chest — collapse, pneumonia. Day 3 to 5: wound infection, anastomotic leak. Day 5 to 10: clots in the legs and lungs, dehiscence. A fever on day 2 usually comes from the chest, shallow breathing because it hurts, so parts of the lung collapse; later fever points to the wound, the urine, a drip site or a leak inside the abdomen. Pain relief is a chest treatment: a patient with an abdominal wound will not breathe deeply, cough or get out of bed while it hurts. The wound that leaks clear pink fluid around day 5 to 8 often precedes the deeper layers giving way — report it. If the wound bursts open: do not push anything back inside; cover with a sterile dressing soaked in warm saline; lie her down with knees bent; nil by mouth, call the surgeon, stay with her.",
